WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.641 --> 00:00:10.168
The single word names a revolution in communications and computer technology.

2
00:00:10.829 --> 00:00:12.430
Emails arrive almost instantly.

3
00:00:13.151 --> 00:00:17.054
Online shoppers order everything from books to cars without leaving their homes.

4
00:00:17.734 --> 00:00:19.876
Families share photos from across the world.

5
00:00:23.179 --> 00:00:28.243
Massive amounts of information flow from one part of the world to another, as easily as water flows

6
00:00:28.283 --> 00:00:31.121
downhill. The Internet has changed our lives.

7
00:00:31.741 --> 00:00:36.564
But how did this complex system of billions of webpages, which are viewed by millions of people every

8
00:00:36.624 --> 00:00:37.865
hour of every day, begin?

9
00:00:40.886 --> 00:00:46.450
The Internet is made up of many computer networks, and it began in 1969 with the network called

10
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:53.253
ARPANET. ARPA stands for the Advanced Research Projects Agency, which is part of the United States Department of

11
00:00:53.313 --> 00:00:57.856
Defense. ARPA was responsible for organizing the defense department's research.

12
00:01:00.028 --> 00:01:04.371
ARPA needed a way to send and receive vast amounts of information all over the United States.

13
00:01:05.271 --> 00:01:11.895
Unfortunately each of the universities and businesses doing research for ARPA had its own computer system; these systems

14
00:01:11.935 --> 00:01:13.436
could not communicate with each other.

15
00:01:14.236 --> 00:01:18.779
ARPA decided to solve this problem by creating standard rules for these computers to communicate.

16
00:01:21.601 --> 00:01:26.964
The result was ARPANET, which was the first computer network to link computers over long distances.

17
00:01:27.696 --> 00:01:34.139
Eventually universities started to use this network for their own purposes, and it was not long before university

18
00:01:34.179 --> 00:01:37.480
students as well as professors had their own email addresses.

19
00:01:38.341 --> 00:01:42.843
As the network grew and more and more computers were connected, it became the Internet.

20
00:01:45.784 --> 00:01:49.306
The communication standards developed by ARPA are called protocols.

21
00:01:49.926 --> 00:01:52.567
The word "protocol" comes from the world of diplomacy.

22
00:01:53.248 --> 00:02:00.501
In diplomacy, following protocol means following the precise rules for meeting or communicating with governments, their ambassadors, and

23
00:02:00.541 --> 00:02:01.082
their rulers.

24
00:02:04.344 --> 00:02:08.928
ARPA invented the protocols that allow computers to exchange information over the Internet.

25
00:02:09.689 --> 00:02:15.854
Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) checks that every piece of information a computer sends is complete.

26
00:02:16.714 --> 00:02:22.319
It is TCP that stops a computer from getting confused about what information is an email and what

27
00:02:22.399 --> 00:02:25.744
is a webpage.

28
00:02:25.764 --> 00:02:31.908
Internet Protocol (IP) ensures that every transmission is sent via the correct route and that the right data

29
00:02:31.988 --> 00:02:33.309
arrives at the right computer.

30
00:02:34.089 --> 00:02:39.573
Not all computers are connected all the time, and often they receive more data than they can handle.

31
00:02:40.253 --> 00:02:44.976
For data traveling on the Internet, this can be like finding a road blocked or getting stuck in

32
00:02:45.016 --> 00:02:45.716
a traffic jam.

33
00:02:46.417 --> 00:02:51.400
But Internet Protocol makes sure that every piece of data takes the fastest route to its destination.

34
00:02:53.716 --> 00:02:59.578
In its early years, the Internet was used only by scientists and other academics and was limited to

35
00:02:59.639 --> 00:03:03.020
a few simple functions, such as email and file transfers.

36
00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:10.663
As more universities linked their computers together, researchers wanted a way to access one online document directly from

37
00:03:10.743 --> 00:03:18.326
another. It was a difficult task; a system was needed to send any file to any computer in

38
00:03:18.366 --> 00:03:22.408
the world, and then automatically display the needed information.

39
00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:30.785
Tim Berners-Lee was a computer programmer at the European Organization for Nuclear Research (CERN) in Switzerland.

40
00:03:34.048 --> 00:03:39.271
He wanted to make the Internet easier to use and help researchers share information.

41
00:03:39.371 --> 00:03:47.217
In 1989 he invented the Uniform Resource Locator (URL) and HyperText Markup Language (HTML).

42
00:03:48.038 --> 00:03:55.222
HTML is the language used to write webpages, and URLs indicate the location of files on the Internet.

43
00:03:57.803 --> 00:04:02.306
By creating these two tools, Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web.

44
00:04:03.186 --> 00:04:06.468
He created the first website, and it is still online.

45
00:04:07.208 --> 00:04:40.654
You can see it for yourself by clicking on its URL, which is http://www.w3.org/ The first webpages contained

46
00:04:40.874 --> 00:04:41.494
only text.

47
00:04:42.255 --> 00:04:46.718
While a text link on a page could let you download an image to your computer, there was

48
00:04:46.778 --> 00:04:50.556
no easy way to display an image on a webpage.

49
00:04:50.576 --> 00:04:56.740
This changed in 1991, when Marc Andreessen created Mosaic, the first user-friendly Web browser.

50
00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:00.562
He went on to found the company Netscape in 1993.

51
00:05:02.243 --> 00:05:06.045
Netscape sold a Web browser called Navigator, and it was a great success.

52
00:05:06.966 --> 00:05:11.809
It was so successful that Microsoft built its own browser called Internet Explorer.

53
00:05:12.609 --> 00:05:17.392
Microsoft included its browser for free with every copy of the Windows operating system.

54
00:05:18.388 --> 00:05:25.613
Netscape complained that this giveaway was unfair competition, but the free copies of Internet Explorer gave almost everyone

55
00:05:25.693 --> 00:05:27.194
an easy way to go online.

56
00:05:29.816 --> 00:05:37.461
With both Andreessen's browser and Berners-Lee's Web protocols, anyone could visit a webpage and, more importantly, create one.

57
00:05:38.102 --> 00:05:42.064
By 1993 more than 800,000 webpages had been created.

58
00:05:42.845 --> 00:05:47.248
In August 2005 over twenty billion webpages were available online.

59
00:05:50.116 --> 00:05:53.679
Use of the Internet and the World Wide Web keeps increasing every year.

60
00:05:54.479 --> 00:06:00.563
In 2005 almost 730 million people had access to the Internet according to one estimate.

61
00:06:01.244 --> 00:06:05.326
Faster Internet speeds have also increased public and commercial interest.

62
00:06:07.368 --> 00:06:12.151
So many people are online that companies are spending billions of dollars advertising on webpages.

63
00:06:12.871 --> 00:06:17.815
There is so much information on the Internet that helping people search the Internet created its two biggest

64
00:06:17.895 --> 00:06:19.636
companies, Google and Yahoo!

65
00:06:21.132 --> 00:06:24.514
The Internet is also replacing long-established technologies.

66
00:06:25.415 --> 00:06:28.197
To a great extent, email has replaced letter writing.

67
00:06:28.958 --> 00:06:33.941
By connecting a headphone and microphone to their computers, people can speak with each other over the Internet,

68
00:06:34.281 --> 00:06:35.802
replacing the need for a telephone.

69
00:06:36.683 --> 00:06:41.406
Video chatting is allowing people to meet online, instead of having to fly long distances.

70
00:06:44.148 --> 00:06:45.829
The Internet has changed our lives.

71
00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.512
We count on it for business and personal communication.

72
00:06:50.624 --> 00:06:52.425
We turn to it for news and entertainment.

73
00:06:53.386 --> 00:06:57.268
In the few short years since its invention, it has reshaped modern life.

74
00:06:58.289 --> 00:07:04.012
With faster and faster speeds and new wireless technologies, who knows where it will take us tomorrow?
